(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "9.6", title: "AI, ethics and a capstone project", q: "What can machines learn, and what should we let them decide?", lessons: [
{ t: "What is AI? Rules versus learning", o: "I can explain what artificial intelligence is and compare rule-based and learning systems.",
  k: ["artificial intelligence (AI): computer systems that carry out tasks that normally need human intelligence", "rule-based system: a program that follows rules written by people", "machine learning: a system that improves at a task by finding patterns in data"],
  d: ["Name three things you have used this week that might involve AI.", "For example recommendations, a voice assistant, photo face grouping, translation, predictive text, a chatbot."],
  s: ["What counts as AI?|Recognising speech and images|Translating, recommending, predicting|Playing games, driving, holding conversations",
      "Two approaches|Rule-based: people write every rule, 'if temperature < 18 then heater on'|Machine learning: the system finds the rules from examples|Many real systems combine both",
      "Strengths and limits|Rules are clear and predictable, but miss situations nobody thought of|Learning handles messy data, but can be hard to explain|Neither 'thinks' or 'understands' like a person"],
  a: "Sort the twelve systems into rule-based or machine learning with a reason, then write five rules that recognise a cat in a photo and find a picture that breaks each rule. Support: sort eight systems. Stretch: explain why rule-based systems struggle with tasks such as recognising handwriting.",
  w: [["What is artificial intelligence?", "Computer systems that carry out tasks that normally need human intelligence."], ["What is a rule-based system?", "A program that follows rules written by people."], ["What is machine learning?", "A system that improves at a task by finding patterns in data."], ["Is a thermostat that follows 'if below 18 then heat' rule-based or learning?", "Rule-based."], ["Explain why writing rules to recognise a cat in any photo is so difficult.", "Cats appear in endless poses, colours, lighting and backgrounds; any fixed rule has exceptions."]],
  x: ["Does an AI system understand what it is doing in the way a person does?", "No. It finds and applies patterns."] },
{ t: "Machine learning and training data", o: "I can describe how a model learns from training data and name types of machine learning.",
  k: ["training data: the examples a model learns from", "model: what a machine learning system produces: the learned patterns it uses to make predictions", "label: the correct answer attached to a training example"],
  d: ["How did you learn to tell a dog from a cat as a small child?", "From seeing many examples and being told which was which."],
  s: ["How learning works|Give the model many examples|It adjusts itself to reduce its mistakes|Test it on examples it has never seen",
      "Types of machine learning|Supervised: examples come with labels, 'cat' or 'dog'|Unsupervised: no labels; the model finds groups by itself|Reinforcement: the model tries actions and gets rewards or penalties",
      "Neural networks|Layers of connected nodes: input, hidden, output|Each connection has a weight that is adjusted during training|More layers can learn more complex patterns: deep learning"],
  a: "Classify six real systems by type of machine learning. Then label the diagram of a simple neural network and explain what happens during training. Support: use the definitions to match the systems. Stretch: explain why a model must be tested on data it was not trained on.",
  w: [["What is training data?", "The examples a model learns from."], ["What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?", "Supervised learning uses labelled examples; unsupervised learning finds groups in data without labels."], ["A game-playing program improves by winning and losing thousands of games. Which type of learning is this?", "Reinforcement learning."], ["Name the three kinds of layer in a neural network.", "Input, hidden and output."], ["Explain why a model is tested on data it has not seen before.", "To check it has learned general patterns, not just memorised the training examples."]],
  x: ["What is a label in training data?", "The correct answer attached to an example."] },
{ t: "Lab: train a classifier and find where it fails", o: "I can train a simple model and investigate its limits.",
  k: ["classifier: a model that sorts inputs into categories", "accuracy: the proportion of predictions that are correct", "confidence: how sure the model is about a prediction"],
  d: ["You train a model only on photos of white cats. What might happen when it sees a black cat?", "It may fail to recognise it as a cat."],
  s: ["Train|Choose two or three categories, such as hand signs|Give 20 or more examples of each|Train the model in the browser tool",
      "Test|Try new examples it has not seen|Record the prediction and the confidence|Count how many are correct",
      "Find the failures|Change the lighting, background or angle|Use examples unlike the training data|Explain each failure using the training data"],
  a: "Train a classifier with the browser tool, test it on 20 new examples and record the accuracy. Then deliberately find three failures and explain each. Retrain with better data and measure again. Support: use the prepared two-category project. Stretch: show how removing variety from the training data lowers accuracy.",
  w: [["What is a classifier?", "A model that sorts inputs into categories."], ["Your model got 16 out of 20 test examples right. What is its accuracy?", "80%."], ["Describe one failure you found and its cause.", "A failure linked to a gap in the training data."], ["What did you change to improve the model, and what happened to the accuracy?", "A change to the training data and the new accuracy."], ["Explain why more varied training data usually gives a better model.", "The model sees more of the situations it will meet, so its patterns work for more new examples."]],
  x: ["What was your model's accuracy before and after retraining?", "Two percentages."] },
{ t: "Bias in data: case studies", o: "I can explain how bias in training data leads to unfair results.",
  k: ["bias: an unfair tendency in a system's results towards or against a group", "representative: including all the kinds of examples the system will meet", "fairness: treating people equally, without unjustified differences"],
  d: ["A face-unlock system was trained mostly on photos of adults. Who might it fail for?", "Children, and anyone who looks unlike most of the training photos."],
  s: ["Where bias comes from|Training data that is not representative|Past decisions that were themselves unfair|Labels chosen by people with their own assumptions",
      "Effects|Lower accuracy for some groups|Unfair decisions about jobs, loans or policing|The bias can look objective because a computer produced it",
      "Reducing bias|Check that training data represents everyone affected|Test accuracy for each group separately|Keep a person responsible for important decisions"],
  a: "Analyse three fictional case studies: identify the source of bias, who was harmed and one way to reduce it. Then discuss: should a model be used if it is accurate overall but much less accurate for one group? Support: use the guiding questions on each case card. Stretch: design a test to check a hiring tool for bias.",
  w: [["What is bias in an AI system?", "An unfair tendency in its results towards or against a group."], ["Give two sources of bias.", "Any two: unrepresentative training data, past unfair decisions in the data, biased labels."], ["What does representative training data mean?", "It includes all the kinds of examples the system will meet."], ["Why should accuracy be tested separately for each group?", "A good overall accuracy can hide poor accuracy for a smaller group."], ["Explain why biased results from a computer can be more harmful than one biased person.", "The system may make many decisions quickly, and people may trust it as objective."]],
  x: ["Give one way to reduce bias in an AI system.", "For example use representative data, test each group, keep human oversight."] },
{ t: "Expert systems", o: "I can describe the parts of an expert system.",
  k: ["expert system: a program that gives advice using knowledge captured from human experts", "knowledge base: the facts the system knows about its subject", "inference engine: the part that applies the rules to the facts to reach conclusions"],
  d: ["A doctor asks you a series of questions before diagnosing an illness. How could a computer do something similar?", "Ask the same questions and apply rules from experts to the answers."],
  s: ["The parts|Knowledge base: facts about the subject|Rule base: IF ... THEN rules from experts|Inference engine: applies the rules to the user's answers|User interface: asks questions and gives the advice",
      "Example|Rule: IF fever AND rash THEN possible measles|The user answers yes to fever and to rash|The inference engine reaches the conclusion and explains why",
      "Uses and limits|Medical diagnosis, car fault finding, plant identification|Consistent and always available|Only as good as its rules; no common sense"],
  a: "Label the parts of an expert system diagram, then follow a printed plant-identification expert system with three plants and record the rules used. Support: use the labelled diagram cards. Stretch: explain one advantage and one risk of using an expert system instead of a human expert.",
  w: [["What is an expert system?", "A program that gives advice using knowledge captured from human experts."], ["What does the knowledge base contain?", "Facts about the subject."], ["What does the inference engine do?", "Applies the rules to the facts and the user's answers to reach conclusions."], ["Name the part of an expert system that asks the user questions.", "The user interface."], ["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of an expert system.", "Advantage: consistent and available at any time. Disadvantage: limited to its rules and has no common sense."]],
  x: ["Name the four parts of an expert system.", "Knowledge base, rule base, inference engine, user interface."] },
{ t: "Lab: build a paper expert system", o: "I can build and test a simple expert system as a decision tree.",
  k: ["decision tree: a branching set of yes or no questions that leads to a conclusion", "rule: an IF ... THEN statement", "leaf: an end point of a decision tree, giving the conclusion"],
  d: ["Write a yes or no question that splits animals into two groups.", "For example: does it have feathers?"],
  s: ["Choose a subject|Identify a fruit, a sport, a pet, or a computer fault|8 to 12 possible conclusions|Each needs a way to tell it apart",
      "Build|Write yes or no questions that split the group|Draw them as a tree|Every route ends at one conclusion",
      "Test and turn into rules|Ask a partner to use it with something they are thinking of|Fix any route that gives a wrong answer|Write each route as an IF ... THEN rule"],
  a: "Build a decision tree for your subject with at least eight conclusions, test it with three people and write its rules. Support: complete the part-built fruit tree. Stretch: write the rules as Python if-elif statements.",
  w: [["What is a decision tree?", "A branching set of yes or no questions that leads to a conclusion."], ["What subject did you choose, and how many conclusions does your tree have?", "A subject and a number."], ["Write one route from your tree as an IF ... THEN rule.", "A rule with AND-joined conditions and a conclusion."], ["What went wrong when your tree was tested, and how did you fix it?", "A specific problem and fix."], ["Explain how your decision tree relates to the parts of an expert system.", "The questions are the user interface, the routes are the rules, and following them is what the inference engine does."]],
  x: ["How many questions does the longest route in your tree ask?", "A number."] },
{ t: "Automated systems and robotics", o: "I can describe automated systems and robots and their advantages and disadvantages.",
  k: ["automated system: a system that uses sensors, a microprocessor and actuators to work without a person controlling it", "robot: a programmable machine that can carry out physical tasks", "actuator: a device that causes movement or action, such as a motor or valve"],
  d: ["Last year you built a micro:bit device that sensed and responded. What were its three parts?", "Input (sensor), processing (microprocessor), output (actuator or display)."],
  s: ["Automated systems|Sensor measures, such as temperature|Microprocessor compares with stored values and decides|Actuator acts, such as switching a heater|The loop repeats continuously",
      "Robots|Factory arms, warehouse robots, surgical robots|Domestic: robot vacuums|Some are controlled by people; some are autonomous",
      "Advantages and disadvantages|Work all day without tiring; accurate and consistent; safe in danger|Expensive to buy and maintain|Can replace jobs; struggle with situations they were not designed for"],
  a: "Describe four automated systems (traffic lights, a greenhouse, an automatic door, a self-checkout) as sensor, decision and actuator. Then complete a table of advantages and disadvantages of robots in three industries. Support: describe two systems. Stretch: explain what changes when an automated system also uses machine learning.",
  w: [["What is an automated system?", "A system that uses sensors, a microprocessor and actuators to work without a person controlling it."], ["What is an actuator?", "A device that causes movement or action, such as a motor or valve."], ["Describe how an automated greenhouse keeps the temperature correct.", "A temperature sensor sends readings to a microprocessor, which compares them with stored values and switches a heater or window motor on or off; this repeats continuously."], ["Give two advantages of using robots in a factory.", "Any two: work continuously, consistent quality, accurate, safer for dangerous tasks, lower long-term costs."], ["Give two disadvantages.", "Any two: expensive to set up and maintain, can replace workers, cannot easily adapt to new tasks, need skilled technicians."]],
  x: ["Name the three parts of an automated system.", "Sensor, microprocessor, actuator."] },
{ t: "Ethics: who is responsible when automation goes wrong?", o: "I can discuss ethical questions raised by automation and AI.",
  k: ["ethics: ideas about what is right and wrong", "accountability: being responsible for a decision and its results", "stakeholder: anyone affected by a decision"],
  d: ["A self-driving car makes a mistake and causes an accident. Who do you think is responsible?", "Open answers: the maker, the programmer, the owner, the passenger, or several of them. That is the debate."],
  s: ["Questions to ask|Who is affected: the stakeholders?|Who is accountable if it goes wrong?|Is it fair to everyone affected?",
      "Areas of debate|Jobs: which disappear, and which new ones appear?|Privacy: what data is collected to make the system work?|Decisions about people: loans, jobs, exam grades|Environment: energy used to train large models",
      "Arguing well|Make a claim|Give evidence or an example|Consider the strongest point on the other side"],
  a: "In groups, analyse one scenario (self-driving cars, AI marking exams, facial recognition in shops, or robots in care homes): list the stakeholders, the benefits, the risks and who is accountable. Prepare arguments for both sides. Support: use the stakeholder map template. Stretch: propose a rule that would make the system more acceptable, and its weakness.",
  w: [["What does accountability mean?", "Being responsible for a decision and its results."], ["What is a stakeholder?", "Anyone affected by a decision."], ["List three stakeholders in your scenario.", "Three relevant stakeholders."], ["Give one benefit and one risk of your scenario.", "A benefit and a risk."], ["Explain why it can be hard to decide who is accountable when an AI system makes a mistake.", "Many people are involved, the system's reasoning may be hard to explain, and it may have learned the behaviour from data nobody checked."]],
  x: ["Which side of your debate has the stronger evidence, in your opinion?", "A reasoned view."] },
{ t: "Lab: structured debate", o: "I can argue a position with evidence and respond to the other side.",
  k: ["motion: the statement being debated", "rebuttal: a response that challenges the other side's point", "evidence: facts or examples that support a claim"],
  d: ["What is the difference between an opinion and an argument?", "An argument gives reasons and evidence; an opinion may not."],
  s: ["The motion|'This house believes AI should never make decisions about people without a human checking'|Two teams: proposing and opposing|Judges score each speaker",
      "Format|Opening speeches: two minutes each|Rebuttals: one minute each|Questions from the floor, then closing speeches",
      "Scoring|Clear claim|Evidence or example|Responds to the other side|Respectful"],
  a: "Take part in the structured debate in your assigned role, then write a balanced paragraph that gives the strongest argument on each side and your own conclusion. Support: speak from the prepared argument card. Stretch: write a 300-word essay on the motion.",
  w: [["What is a rebuttal?", "A response that challenges the other side's point."], ["Write the strongest argument for the motion.", "A reasoned argument, for example people must remain accountable for decisions affecting lives."], ["Write the strongest argument against the motion.", "A reasoned argument, for example checking every decision is impossible at scale and humans are also biased."], ["Give one piece of evidence used in the debate.", "A specific example or fact."], ["Write your own conclusion with a reason.", "A reasoned conclusion."]],
  x: ["Did the debate change your view? Why or why not?", "A reflection."] },
{ t: "The development life cycle and choosing a project", o: "I can describe the stages of developing a program and choose a project.",
  k: ["analysis: working out exactly what the program must do", "design: planning how the program will work", "iterative: repeating the cycle of building, testing and improving"],
  d: ["Why do professional programmers not just start typing code?", "They need to know what to build and plan it, or they waste time and miss requirements."],
  s: ["The stages|Analysis: what is needed? Requirements and success criteria|Design: structure chart, flowchart or pseudocode, test plan|Coding: build in small parts|Testing: does it meet every requirement?",
      "Iterative development|Build a small working part|Test it|Improve it and add the next part",
      "Choose a project|Guided: a quiz with a question file and saved scores|Open: a revision tool, a budget tracker or a text adventure|Free: your own idea, agreed with your teacher"],
  a: "Order the life cycle cards and match each to the work done at that stage. Choose your capstone project and write its requirements: what it must do, for whom, and five success criteria. Support: choose the guided project, which has its requirements started. Stretch: identify the biggest risk to finishing your project and how you will manage it.",
  w: [["Name the four stages of the development life cycle.", "Analysis, design, coding, testing (accept evaluation)."], ["What happens in the analysis stage?", "Working out exactly what the program must do."], ["What does iterative development mean?", "Building, testing and improving in repeated small cycles."], ["Which project did you choose?", "Guided, open or free, with a title."], ["Write two of your success criteria.", "Two testable statements."]],
  x: ["Why are success criteria written before any code?", "So the program can be built to them and tested against them."] },
{ t: "Analysis and design", o: "I can produce a design for my program.",
  k: ["decomposition: breaking a problem into smaller parts", "structure chart: a diagram of a program's subroutines", "test plan: a list of tests, with test data and expected results"],
  d: ["Your project needs to save data between runs. Which Python feature from Unit 9.1 will you use?", "Text files."],
  s: ["Decompose|Break the project into subroutines|Each with one job, its parameters and its return value|Draw the structure chart",
      "Design the logic|Flowchart or pseudocode for the main loop|Pseudocode for the hardest subroutine|List the variables, lists and files",
      "Plan the tests|Normal, boundary and abnormal data for every input|Expected result for each|At least eight tests"],
  a: "Complete your design pack: structure chart, pseudocode for the main loop and one subroutine, data table and test plan. Get it checked by a partner before building. Support: use the design pack template. Stretch: identify which parts can be built and tested independently, and plan the order.",
  w: [["List the subroutines in your structure chart.", "A list."], ["Write pseudocode for your main loop.", "Valid pseudocode."], ["Which data will be stored in a file?", "A description."], ["Write one test with normal data and one with abnormal data.", "Two tests with expected results."], ["Explain how your design breaks the problem into manageable parts.", "Each subroutine has one job and can be built and tested on its own."]],
  x: ["Which subroutine will you build first?", "A named subroutine with a reason."] },
{ t: "Lab: build sprint 1", o: "I can build and test the first part of my project.",
  k: ["sprint: a short, focused period of building", "version control: keeping saved versions so you can go back", "unit test: testing one subroutine on its own"],
  d: ["What should you do after finishing each subroutine?", "Test it on its own, then save a version."],
  s: ["Build the core|The main loop and the most important subroutine|Leave extras until later|Comment each subroutine",
      "Test as you go|Test each subroutine with your planned data|Fix before moving on|Record results in the test plan",
      "Save versions|Save as project_v1, project_v2 and so on|If something breaks badly, go back|Note what changed in each version"],
  a: "Build and test the core of your project, recording test results and saving a version at the end of the lesson. Support: build from the guided project starter. Stretch: write automatic tests that print True or False for each subroutine.",
  w: [["What did you build today?", "A description."], ["Record one unit test and its result.", "A complete test."], ["Describe one bug you fixed.", "A bug and its fix."], ["What version number did you save?", "A version."], ["What will you build in the next sprint?", "The next parts from the plan."]],
  x: ["Which success criteria does your project already meet?", "A list."] },
{ t: "Build sprint 2", o: "I can continue building my project and keep it working.",
  k: ["integration: joining parts of a program together", "regression: a working feature that breaks after a change", "priority: what matters most to finish"],
  d: ["You added a new feature and an old one stopped working. What is this called, and how would you notice?", "A regression. By rerunning the earlier tests."],
  s: ["Add features|Next priority from your plan|Integrate it with the main loop|Keep each subroutine to one job",
      "Rerun earlier tests|Every new feature can break an old one|Rerun the tests that passed before|Fix regressions straight away",
      "Check progress|Tick off success criteria|Are you on track to finish?|Decide what to leave out if needed"],
  a: "Add the next features from your plan, rerun all earlier tests and update the test plan. Save a new version. Support: add one feature with the teacher's help card. Stretch: add file saving and loading if it is not done yet.",
  w: [["What features did you add?", "A list."], ["Did any earlier test fail after your changes?", "Yes or no, with details."], ["What is integration?", "Joining parts of a program together."], ["Which success criteria are now met?", "A list."], ["If you are behind, what will you leave out, and why?", "A prioritised decision."]],
  x: ["What percentage of your plan is complete?", "An estimate."] },
{ t: "Testing with normal, boundary and abnormal data", o: "I can test my program thoroughly and record evidence.",
  k: ["test evidence: a record of tests and their results", "abnormal data: data that should be rejected", "boundary data: data at the edge of what is accepted"],
  d: ["Your program accepts a menu choice from 1 to 4. Give normal, boundary and abnormal test data.", "Normal 2; boundary 1 and 4 (or 0 and 5); abnormal 9 or 'x'."],
  s: ["Test every input|Normal data: should work|Boundary data: on and just outside each limit|Abnormal data: wrong type or out of range",
      "Record|Test number, purpose, test data, expected, actual, pass or fail|Screenshot or copy the output as evidence|Be honest about failures",
      "Fix and retest|Fix one failure at a time|Rerun the failed test|Then rerun the others"],
  a: "Carry out your full test plan, recording expected and actual results with evidence. Fix failures and retest. Support: test the three main inputs. Stretch: ask a partner to try to break your program and add their tests to the plan.",
  w: [["What is abnormal data?", "Data that should be rejected."], ["Give boundary data for an input that accepts 1 to 10.", "1 and 10 (and 0 and 11 just outside)."], ["Record one test that failed and how you fixed it.", "A failed test and its fix."], ["Why should test evidence include the actual output?", "To prove the result, not just claim it."], ["Explain why a partner trying to break your program is a useful test.", "They try inputs and actions you did not think of."]],
  x: ["How many tests passed out of how many?", "Two numbers."] },
{ t: "Lab: build sprint 3 and fix defects", o: "I can finish my project and fix remaining defects.",
  k: ["defect: a fault that makes the program behave wrongly", "polish: improve usability and appearance once it works", "release: the finished version handed in"],
  d: ["Which should come first: fixing a crash or improving the welcome message?", "Fixing the crash."],
  s: ["Fix defects first|Crashes, then wrong results, then usability|Retest after each fix|Update the test plan",
      "Polish|Clear prompts and messages|Comments for every subroutine|Meaningful names throughout",
      "Prepare the release|Final version saved and named clearly|All files included, such as data files|Run it from scratch once more"],
  a: "Fix remaining defects in priority order, polish the program and save the release version. Run it from scratch to check everything is included. Support: fix the defects your teacher has highlighted. Stretch: add one extra feature from your plan.",
  w: [["What is a defect?", "A fault that makes the program behave wrongly."], ["List the defects you fixed today.", "A list."], ["In what order did you fix them, and why?", "Order by severity, with a reason."], ["What did you do to polish the program?", "Specific improvements."], ["Why run the release version from scratch before handing it in?", "To check nothing is missing, such as a data file, and that it works for someone else."]],
  x: ["Is your release version complete and tested?", "Yes, or what remains."] },
{ t: "Evaluation and documentation", o: "I can evaluate my project against its success criteria and document it.",
  k: ["evaluation: judging how well the solution meets its requirements, with evidence", "documentation: written information that explains how to use and maintain a program", "user guide: instructions for the person using the program"],
  d: ["What is the difference between describing your program and evaluating it?", "Describing says what it does; evaluating judges how well it meets the criteria, with evidence."],
  s: ["Evaluate|Take each success criterion|Met, partly met or not met|Point to test evidence for each",
      "Improve|What would you add or change with more time?|Why would it help the user?|What would you do differently in the process?",
      "Document|A short user guide: how to run and use it|Technical notes: files, subroutines, known limitations|Comments in the code"],
  a: "Write your evaluation against each success criterion with evidence, then write a one-page user guide and technical notes. Support: use the evaluation and user guide frames. Stretch: get a partner to use your program only from your user guide, and improve the guide based on their questions.",
  w: [["Which success criteria did you fully meet? Give evidence for one.", "A list with evidence."], ["Which criterion was not fully met, and why?", "An honest answer."], ["State two specific improvements.", "Two improvements with reasons."], ["What is a user guide?", "Instructions for the person using the program."], ["Explain why technical notes help someone who maintains the program later.", "They explain how it is organised and its limits, so changes can be made safely."]],
  x: ["What one thing would you do differently next time?", "A specific change."] },
{ t: "Assessed showcase", o: "I can present my project and explain how it works.",
  k: ["showcase: presenting finished work to an audience", "demonstrate: show it working", "justify: give reasons for design decisions"],
  d: ["Choose the two parts of your code you are proudest of. Why those?", "The student's own choice and reasons."],
  s: ["The showcase|What does it do and who is it for?|Live demonstration|Three minutes each",
      "Explain the code|One subroutine: parameters, return value, purpose|How data is stored: lists or files|One decision you made and why",
      "How you are assessed|The project rubric: design, code, testing, evaluation|Your portfolio|Your explanation"],
  a: "Present your project, answer questions, and hand in the portfolio: requirements, design, code, test evidence, evaluation and user guide. Support: use the presentation prompt card. Stretch: explain how you would extend the project into an IGCSE-level solution.",
  w: [["What does your project do and who is it for?", "A clear description."], ["Explain one subroutine: its parameters, return value and purpose.", "An accurate explanation."], ["How does your program store data?", "Lists and/or files, explained."], ["Describe one design decision and why you made it.", "A decision with a reason."], ["What question were you asked, and how did you answer?", "A question and answer."]],
  x: ["Which rubric strand do you think is your strongest?", "Design, code, testing or evaluation."] },
{ t: "Year review, feedback and reflection", o: "I can reflect on my learning across KS3 and set targets for IGCSE.",
  k: ["reflection: thinking about what and how you have learned", "target: a specific thing to work on next", "progress: how much your knowledge and skills have grown"],
  d: ["List one thing you learned in each year of KS3 Computing.", "One item each for Years 7, 8 and 9."],
  s: ["Three years|Year 7: safety, algorithms, Scratch, binary, hardware, first Python|Year 8: loops, networks, web, data, logic, physical computing|Year 9: lists and functions, pseudocode, security, the CPU, databases, AI",
      "Looking ahead|IGCSE Computer Science builds on every unit|Programming and algorithms carry the most weight|Practise programming outside lessons",
      "Your feedback|Read your project feedback|Note your strengths across the year|Choose two targets"],
  a: "Complete the KS3 reflection: rate your confidence in each strand, write about your best piece of work, and set two targets for IGCSE with a plan for each. Support: use the reflection grid. Stretch: write advice for next year's Year 9 students.",
  w: [["Copy your project feedback and say what you will do with it.", "Feedback and an action."], ["Which KS3 strand is your strongest, and why?", "A reasoned answer."], ["Which strand do you need to strengthen before IGCSE?", "A named strand."], ["Describe your best piece of work this year.", "A specific piece of work."], ["Set two targets for IGCSE with a plan for each.", "Two targets with plans."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, what can machines learn, and what should we let them decide?", "Machines can learn patterns from data, but decisions that affect people need fair data, testing and a responsible person accountable for them."] }
] }); })(typeof module !== "undefined" ? require("../lessons.js") : window.Toolkit);
